# PATENT CLAIM

**Claim 1.** A fuel cell control apparatus comprising:

a fuel cell stack having an anode side and a cathode side;

an anode gas circulation system configured to circulate anode off-gas from the anode side back to an anode inlet, the anode gas circulation system including a circulation flow adjusting device configured to control a circulation flow rate of the anode gas;

a detection unit configured to detect or estimate an operating state parameter of the fuel cell stack, the operating state parameter including at least one of a target output current, a target output power, a stack temperature, and an anode pressure;

a target circulation flow rate calculation unit configured to calculate a target circulation flow rate for the anode gas circulation system based on the operating state parameter detected by the detection unit;

a rate limiter configured to receive the target circulation flow rate and to output a rate-limited target circulation flow rate by restricting a rate of change of the target circulation flow rate to within a predetermined upper limit rate of change and a predetermined lower limit rate of change, such that during a transient operation in which the operating state parameter changes abruptly, an actual change rate of a command value supplied to the circulation flow adjusting device is constrained below the rate of change achievable by the circulation flow adjusting device;

a deficiency calculation unit configured to calculate a control deficiency amount representing a difference between the target circulation flow rate calculated by the target circulation flow rate calculation unit and the rate-limited target circulation flow rate output by the rate limiter;

a priority determination unit configured to select, from among a plurality of manipulated variables affecting the anode gas circulation flow rate or a hydrogen concentration within the anode gas circulation system, at least one lower-priority manipulated variable other than the circulation flow rate adjusting device, wherein the circulation flow rate adjusting device is assigned a higher control priority than the at least one lower-priority manipulated variable; and

a compensation control unit configured to compensate for the control deficiency amount calculated by the deficiency calculation unit by adjusting the at least one lower-priority manipulated variable selected by the priority determination unit, independently of a command value supplied to the circulation flow rate adjusting device,

wherein the circulation flow rate adjusting device is controlled based on the rate-limited target circulation flow rate output by the rate limiter, and the at least one lower-priority manipulated variable is controlled based on the control deficiency amount so as to suppress at least one of a hydrogen concentration deviation and a pressure deviation within the anode gas circulation system that would otherwise result from the restricted rate of change of the target circulation flow rate.

**Claim 2.** The fuel cell control apparatus according to claim 1, wherein the at least one lower-priority manipulated variable comprises at least one of: an anode pressure regulating valve opening degree, a fuel gas supply flow rate from a fuel gas supply source, an anode gas discharge valve opening degree, and a cathode gas flow rate.

**Claim 3.** The fuel cell control apparatus according to claim 1, wherein the compensation control unit is configured to increase a fuel gas supply flow rate from the fuel gas supply source when the control deficiency amount indicates that the rate-limited target circulation flow rate is lower than the target circulation flow rate calculated by the target circulation flow rate calculation unit during an increasing transient of the operating state parameter.

**Claim 4.** The fuel cell control apparatus according to claim 1, wherein the compensation control unit is configured to open an anode gas discharge valve to discharge a portion of the anode off-gas when the control deficiency amount indicates that the rate-limited target circulation flow rate exceeds a capacity for hydrogen dilution corresponding to the target circulation flow rate calculated by the target circulation flow rate calculation unit during a decreasing transient of the operating state parameter.

**Claim 5.** The fuel cell control apparatus according to claim 1, wherein the predetermined upper limit rate of change and the predetermined lower limit rate of change are set based on a mechanical response characteristic of the circulation flow adjusting device, the mechanical response characteristic including at least one of a maximum rotational acceleration and a maximum rotational deceleration of a circulation pump.

**Claim 6.** The fuel cell control apparatus according to claim 1, wherein the priority determination unit is configured to select the at least one lower-priority manipulated variable based on the operating state parameter, such that a different lower-priority manipulated variable is selected depending on whether the transient operation corresponds to an increasing change or a decreasing change in the target output current or the target output power.

**Claim 7.** The fuel cell control apparatus according to claim 1, further comprising a hydrogen concentration estimation unit configured to estimate a hydrogen concentration in the anode gas circulation system based on the rate-limited target circulation flow rate, wherein the compensation control unit adjusts the at least one lower-priority manipulated variable further based on the estimated hydrogen concentration so as to maintain the estimated hydrogen concentration within a predetermined allowable range.

**Claim 8.** A method for controlling a fuel cell system, the method comprising:

detecting an operating state parameter of a fuel cell stack;

calculating a target circulation flow rate for an anode gas circulation system based on the detected operating state parameter;

limiting a rate of change of the target circulation flow rate to generate a rate-limited target circulation flow rate;

calculating a control deficiency amount corresponding to a difference between the target circulation flow rate and the rate-limited target circulation flow rate;

controlling a circulation flow adjusting device of the anode gas circulation system based on the rate-limited target circulation flow rate; and

compensating for the control deficiency amount by adjusting at least one manipulated variable having a lower control priority than the circulation flow adjusting device, independently of the circulation flow adjusting device, so as to suppress a hydrogen concentration deviation or a pressure deviation arising from the limited rate of change of the target circulation flow rate.

# Independent Claim: Fuel Cell System

**Claim 1.** A fuel cell system comprising:

a fuel cell configured to generate electric power through an electrochemical reaction between an anode gas supplied to an anode electrode and a cathode gas supplied to a cathode electrode, the fuel cell having an electrolyte membrane whose humidity state affects power generation performance;

an anode gas supply passage configured to supply the anode gas to the fuel cell;

an anode gas circulation passage configured to return an anode off-gas discharged from the fuel cell to the anode gas supply passage;

an anode gas circulation flow rate control unit configured to control a circulation flow rate of the anode gas flowing through the anode gas circulation passage;

a wet/dry state detecting unit configured to detect a wet/dry state of the electrolyte membrane of the fuel cell based on at least one physical quantity among a plurality of physical quantities related to an operating state of the fuel cell system, and to determine whether the electrolyte membrane is in a transition state between a wet state and a dry state; and

a priority setting unit configured to set, when the wet/dry state detecting unit determines that the electrolyte membrane is in the transition state, a priority order among the plurality of physical quantities for controlling the anode gas circulation flow rate control unit, the priority order being set such that a first physical quantity, among the plurality of physical quantities, that requires a first control direction for the circulation flow rate to shift the electrolyte membrane toward the wet state is prioritized over a second physical quantity, among the plurality of physical quantities, that requires a second control direction for the circulation flow rate opposite to the first control direction to shift the electrolyte membrane toward the dry state,

wherein the anode gas circulation flow rate control unit controls the circulation flow rate of the anode gas based on the physical quantity prioritized by the priority setting unit during the transition state, so as to suppress interference between conflicting flow rate requirements arising from the plurality of physical quantities during the transition state of the electrolyte membrane.
